    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to worry about the volatility of your financial statements; if you dump a massive shrinkage expense all at once, it can look like a one-time disaster rather than a chronic issue. The standard accounting approach to handle losses that span multiple periods is to use **accrual accounting** and **estimates**, rather than waiting until the physical count is perfect.\n\nInstead of waiting for the final count, you should estimate the likely shrinkage rate based on historical data or industry averages for similar high-value items. By applying this estimated percentage to your inventory value at the end of each month, you can recognize a portion of the loss gradually. This spreads the expense over the months the loss actually occurred, keeping your monthly COGS and net income figures much smoother and more realistic.\n\nHowever, you must be careful not to over-estimate, as that would artificially lower your profits. The key is to adjust your estimates periodically as your actual shrinkage data comes in. If you find that the actual loss is consistently higher than your estimate, you can then make a cumulative adjustment in the subsequent months to catch up, though this might cause a temporary spike in expenses again.\n\nIt is also worth noting that while this method smooths the accounting",
